WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.575
This briefing comes from Book 11 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:09.100
Zero Trust and Assessment: how an
agency measures its Zero Trust posture

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.550
across every estate it runs, and turns
what the tools find into a program.

4
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:18.231
I'll take four questions: why Zero Trust
is a whole-agency property, what the

5
00:00:18.231 --> 00:00:22.988
mandates and tools actually cover, who
owns the posture, and how to assess it.

6
00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:26.482
The short version: unify
first, assess second,

7
00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:31.000
and treat every tool as
evidence, never as the verdict.

8
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.500
Why is Zero Trust a whole-agency property?

9
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.750
Look at the usual assessment calendar.

10
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.275
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

11
00:00:40.275 --> 00:00:45.275
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

12
00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:48.481
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist,

13
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.687
keyed by whatever the management
plane calls a machine.

14
00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:54.750
At year end someone puts
the three reports behind

15
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.738
one cover page and calls
it the agency's maturity.

16
00:00:57.738 --> 00:01:00.475
It's three partial
assessments with one title.

17
00:01:00.475 --> 00:01:02.425
The failure is architectural.

18
00:01:02.425 --> 00:01:07.804
A database server that runs as a virtual
machine, sits on an agency subnet and backs

19
00:01:07.804 --> 00:01:13.025
up to a cloud bucket can appear as three
unrelated objects, scored by three tools.

20
00:01:13.025 --> 00:01:16.425
A total across them counts
objects, not architecture.

21
00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:19.550
The guidance describes the
enterprise, not a tenant.

22
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.525
Executive Order 14028 says throughout
all aspects of the infrastructure,

23
00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:32.388
and NIST SP 800-207 grants no implicit
trust from network location or ownership.

24
00:01:32.388 --> 00:01:36.270
So unify first, then assess,
and defend the unified plane,

25
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.520
because it becomes a complete
map of the attack surface.

26
00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.386
Two documented incidents
show why the whole-agency

27
00:01:44.386 --> 00:01:47.720
view is a security property,
not a reporting preference.

28
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.778
In May and June 2023, an actor
used a Microsoft consumer

29
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:56.107
signing key to forge tokens
and read Exchange Online mail.

30
00:01:56.107 --> 00:02:01.339
The Cyber Safety Review Board counted
22 enterprise organizations and 503

31
00:02:01.339 --> 00:02:06.820
related personal accounts, with access to
some mailboxes for at least six weeks.

32
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.045
The tokens were valid to every
system that checked them.

33
00:02:10.045 --> 00:02:13.068
What caught the intrusion
was a custom alert one

34
00:02:13.068 --> 00:02:16.620
federal department had built
on mailbox logs it paid for.

35
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.682
The second sits beneath the guest.

36
00:02:18.682 --> 00:02:25.169
ESXiArgs compromised more than
3,800 ESXi servers in February 2023.

37
00:02:25.169 --> 00:02:30.742
In 2024, CVE-2024-37085 gave
full hypervisor control to

38
00:02:30.742 --> 00:02:37.057
members of a directory group
named ESX Admins, matched by name.

39
00:02:37.057 --> 00:02:39.619
Even one vendor's two tools don't add up.

40
00:02:39.619 --> 00:02:43.753
ScubaGear is CISA's, the Zero
Trust Assessment is Microsoft's,

41
00:02:43.753 --> 00:02:46.670
and neither sees past
Microsoft's platform.

42
00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.382
Tools are evidence inputs.

43
00:02:48.382 --> 00:02:50.900
The agency owns the verdict.

44
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.300
Chapter 02 explains why programs
stall at Initial or low Advanced.

45
00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.375
They read CISA's maturity model
as a five-pillar shopping list.

46
00:03:00.375 --> 00:03:04.728
But the model runs those pillars through
three cross-cutting capabilities,

47
00:03:04.728 --> 00:03:08.736
and the one programs fund least is
Governance, which the model defines

48
00:03:08.736 --> 00:03:12.813
as the definition and enforcement of
policy within and across pillars.

49
00:03:12.813 --> 00:03:15.958
Its Traditional stage reads
like the failure list:

50
00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:21.275
siloed pillars of policy enforcement, and
limited correlation of logs and telemetry.

51
00:03:21.275 --> 00:03:22.925
Five principles answer it.

52
00:03:22.925 --> 00:03:26.584
Identity is the anchor, but
it isn't self-authenticating,

53
00:03:26.584 --> 00:03:30.800
so sessions are re-evaluated and
the identity plane is defended.

54
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.573
Privileged identity gets
controls of its own:

55
00:03:33.573 --> 00:03:37.732
Microsoft's guidance is zero
permanently active assignments other

56
00:03:37.732 --> 00:03:41.600
than emergency accounts, which
are tested at least every 90 days.

57
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.813
Evidence must be reproducible,
machine-readable and regenerable.

58
00:03:45.813 --> 00:03:51.650
Governance enforces: a policy that only
produces next quarter's finding is a report.

59
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.645
And the cloud boundary is modeled,

60
00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:58.210
with each government-cloud feature
gap recorded as a governed finding.

61
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.035
What do the mandates actually require?

62
00:04:01.035 --> 00:04:03.698
Four documents get cited
as if they were one.

63
00:04:03.698 --> 00:04:08.352
Executive Order 14028 is binding,
and required each agency

64
00:04:08.352 --> 00:04:12.722
to develop a plan to implement
Zero Trust Architecture.

65
00:04:12.722 --> 00:04:17.935
OMB M-22-09 is binding and sets
specific actions by pillar:

66
00:04:17.935 --> 00:04:23.688
phishing-resistant MFA for staff,
contractors and partners, reliable device

67
00:04:23.688 --> 00:04:27.374
inventories through CDM,
encrypted DNS and HTTPS,

68
00:04:27.374 --> 00:04:30.160
and an environmental isolation plan.

69
00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.173
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model, version 2.0, is guidance.

70
00:04:35.173 --> 00:04:39.883
It says it should not be viewed as a strict
set of requirements but as a general

71
00:04:39.883 --> 00:04:44.298
guide, and CISA's page for it is now
marked archived, with no later version.

72
00:04:44.298 --> 00:04:49.098
NIST SP 800-207 supplies the architecture.

73
00:04:49.098 --> 00:04:54.855
M-22-09's FY 2024 deadlines have
passed, but OMB hasn't rescinded it,

74
00:04:54.855 --> 00:04:58.273
and a passed deadline
doesn't retire an action.

75
00:04:58.273 --> 00:05:04.773
One adjustment: its logging
action pointed to M-21-31,

76
00:05:04.773 --> 00:05:09.309
which M-26-14 rescinded on May 22, 2026.

77
00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:15.220
And no federal document defines a Zero
Trust score, or requires a maturity stage.

78
00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.845
BOD 25-01 is narrower and more concrete.

79
00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:22.570
CISA issued it on December 17, 2024,

80
00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:27.832
for every production cloud tenant
with a finalized SCuBA baseline.

81
00:05:27.832 --> 00:05:33.211
It has six required actions: inventory
the tenants by February 21, 2025;

82
00:05:33.211 --> 00:05:36.642
deploy the SCuBA tools
and report by April 25;

83
00:05:36.642 --> 00:05:41.651
implement the mandatory policies
by June 20; keep up with updates;

84
00:05:41.651 --> 00:05:47.957
baseline every new tenant before its ATO;
and explain deviations with each report.

85
00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:51.346
The mandatory list is a
subset of the baselines.

86
00:05:51.346 --> 00:05:57.648
For Microsoft 365, CISA's Required
Configurations page lists 50 policies;

87
00:05:57.648 --> 00:06:02.020
in Entra ID, that's 20 of the
30 in ScubaGear's baseline.

88
00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:08.582
Google Workspace is now in scope too,
with its policies due December 1, 2026.

89
00:06:08.582 --> 00:06:13.571
CISA's guidance limits scope to software
as a service, so Azure subscriptions are

90
00:06:13.571 --> 00:06:18.558
out even when they share the tenant, and
on-premises Active Directory is untouched.

91
00:06:18.558 --> 00:06:22.083
Inventory, deployment and
explanations take days.

92
00:06:22.083 --> 00:06:27.500
Implementing the policies is architecture,
and that's what the directive exists for.

93
00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:32.849
Agencies run two free, read-only
assessments of their Microsoft cloud,

94
00:06:32.849 --> 00:06:35.862
and often drop one because
it covers identity.

95
00:06:35.862 --> 00:06:37.537
They answer different questions.

96
00:06:37.537 --> 00:06:42.688
ScubaGear is CISA's tool, and asks whether
the tenant meets the federal baseline.

97
00:06:42.688 --> 00:06:50.376
Version 1.8.0 shipped May 7,
2026, with 2.0.0 in preview.

98
00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.411
Microsoft's Zero Trust
Assessment asks how mature

99
00:06:53.411 --> 00:06:56.375
the environment is against
Microsoft's guidance.

100
00:06:56.375 --> 00:07:03.787
Release 3.1.0 shipped October
20, 2026, with 364 tests.

101
00:07:03.787 --> 00:07:07.443
It's a community project
maintained by Microsoft employees,

102
00:07:07.443 --> 00:07:11.537
not an official product, and no
Microsoft support program covers it.

103
00:07:11.537 --> 00:07:18.399
They overlap on Entra ID identity and,
in 3.1.0, on Defender threat policies.

104
00:07:18.399 --> 00:07:23.882
Only ScubaGear covers Exchange Online,
Teams, SharePoint and Power Platform,

105
00:07:23.882 --> 00:07:28.250
and 22 of the 50 mandatory policies
sit in those four products.

106
00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.784
Dropping ScubaGear leaves
mandatory policies unassessed

107
00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:34.612
and stops the reporting
the directive requires.

108
00:07:34.612 --> 00:07:37.348
So run both, and reconcile
at the control level:

109
00:07:37.348 --> 00:07:43.400
the tenant setting and the SP
800-53 control it supports.

110
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:48.350
Everything so far stops at
the edge of Microsoft 365.

111
00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:53.034
Nothing on BOD 25-01's list
covers infrastructure accounts,

112
00:07:53.034 --> 00:07:56.000
the agency network or
the virtualized estate.

113
00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.674
So the whole-agency argument
rests on the Zero Trust documents,

114
00:07:59.674 --> 00:08:05.750
and on the SP 800-53 controls
every system security plan asserts.

115
00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.300
Three things get reconciled
before assessing.

116
00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:14.074
One inventory: CM-8 forbids
duplicate accounting of components,

117
00:08:14.074 --> 00:08:18.650
and BOD 23-01 requires asset
discovery every 7 days.

118
00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.231
One naming authority,

119
00:08:20.231 --> 00:08:24.975
because each cloud provider and
VMware ships its own private DNS.

120
00:08:24.975 --> 00:08:28.883
And no implicit trust from any
boundary: a site-to-site VPN,

121
00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:34.287
a default virtual network and a flat
virtualized segment are the same mistake.

122
00:08:34.287 --> 00:08:38.074
The hypervisor is a distinct
threat class, so its management

123
00:08:38.074 --> 00:08:42.163
plane is assessed as its own surface,
not inferred from its guests.

124
00:08:42.163 --> 00:08:45.162
And every provider's tool
answers for its own platform.

125
00:08:45.162 --> 00:08:49.574
AWS says its NIST standard
supports a subset of requirements.

126
00:08:49.574 --> 00:08:53.612
Microsoft calls Azure Policy
compliance only a partial view.

127
00:08:53.612 --> 00:08:59.110
FedRAMP certifies the service; the
agency authorizes its own use of it.

128
00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:00.710
Who owns it?

129
00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:02.797
Every pillar already has an owner.

130
00:09:02.797 --> 00:09:06.372
What most agencies lack is an
owner for the posture itself.

131
00:09:06.372 --> 00:09:12.508
M-22-09 required each agency to designate
a zero trust strategy implementation

132
00:09:12.508 --> 00:09:16.572
lead within 30 days, and many will
give this role to that person.

133
00:09:16.572 --> 00:09:21.213
The program lead owns the definition of
the whole-agency score, what it covers

134
00:09:21.213 --> 00:09:26.072
and what it excludes, plus cross-pillar
correlation and the assessment calendar.

135
00:09:26.072 --> 00:09:29.113
The lead decides priority
but doesn't accept risk,

136
00:09:29.113 --> 00:09:33.934
and shouldn't also own the agency's
relationship with its largest cloud vendor.

137
00:09:33.934 --> 00:09:36.760
The CIO decides scope and sequence.

138
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.693
The CISO sets the evidence
standard and approves

139
00:09:39.693 --> 00:09:41.997
the tools and the accounts that run them.

140
00:09:41.997 --> 00:09:45.098
The authorizing official
accepts residual risk

141
00:09:45.098 --> 00:09:48.123
and approves each plan of
action and milestones.

142
00:09:48.123 --> 00:09:49.797
Exceptions split on time.

143
00:09:49.797 --> 00:09:53.196
An omission with an expiration
date is a CISO decision;

144
00:09:53.196 --> 00:09:58.072
one with no end date is a standing risk
acceptance for the authorizing official.

145
00:09:58.072 --> 00:10:02.920
Pillar owners decide how to fix a finding,
never that it doesn't need fixing.

146
00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:04.695
How do you get there?

147
00:10:04.695 --> 00:10:07.020
First, close before you assess.

148
00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:10.779
Agencies met BOD 25-01's early dates:

149
00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:15.195
tenants inventoried, ScubaGear
deployed and reporting.

150
00:10:15.195 --> 00:10:17.195
Many now treat that as the answer.

151
00:10:17.195 --> 00:10:20.530
But ScubaGear measures
deviation from a baseline;

152
00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:24.658
it doesn't close any, and neither
does the Zero Trust Assessment.

153
00:10:24.658 --> 00:10:28.708
A thermometer that reports a fever
every day isn't a treatment plan.

154
00:10:28.708 --> 00:10:33.157
So the program's six-phase sequence
puts the assessors at step five,

155
00:10:33.157 --> 00:10:37.850
as confirmation rather than discovery:
understand, design, implement,

156
00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.219
govern, then validate, and then
the authorizing official decides.

157
00:10:42.219 --> 00:10:45.868
That's the Risk Management
Framework's own order:

158
00:10:45.868 --> 00:10:48.695
implement, assess, authorize, monitor.

159
00:10:48.695 --> 00:10:53.823
Before validating, build the layers a
finding depends on: naming and certificates,

160
00:10:53.823 --> 00:10:55.708
device and workload identity,

161
00:10:55.708 --> 00:11:00.232
a phased retirement of legacy
authentication, and governed exceptions.

162
00:11:00.232 --> 00:11:04.367
Meanwhile, route every current
finding to exactly one response:

163
00:11:04.367 --> 00:11:08.971
a configuration change now, architectural
work with an owner and a date,

164
00:11:08.971 --> 00:11:12.170
or a risk acceptance by
the authorizing official.

165
00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:15.540
A finding with no response
is the only failure.

166
00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.127
Then run the assessment
and keep the evidence.

167
00:11:19.127 --> 00:11:23.141
Five things are decided before either
tool touches a production tenant.

168
00:11:23.141 --> 00:11:27.590
Scope comes from the BOD
25-01 tenant inventory.

169
00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:31.968
The host is a dedicated Windows machine
inside the authorization boundary,

170
00:11:31.968 --> 00:11:35.790
a separate one for each tool,
because their dependencies conflict.

171
00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:40.415
Each tool gets a reader account, with
consent granted once, separately.

172
00:11:40.415 --> 00:11:42.703
Output is handled as controlled data.

173
00:11:42.703 --> 00:11:48.173
And the assessment accounts belong
in the M-26-14 Agency Logging Plan,

174
00:11:48.173 --> 00:11:52.877
due 90 days after CISA's August
20 reference architecture.

175
00:11:52.877 --> 00:11:56.428
ScubaGear runs in Windows PowerShell 5.1.

176
00:11:56.428 --> 00:12:01.323
Its configuration file is the exception
record: annotations, omissions with

177
00:12:01.323 --> 00:12:06.065
a rationale and an expiration date,
and exclusions for emergency accounts.

178
00:12:06.065 --> 00:12:08.099
It's versioned in the repository,

179
00:12:08.099 --> 00:12:11.865
and its hash is recorded with
every run, like the tool version.

180
00:12:11.865 --> 00:12:16.940
The results file is never renamed
or edited, only hashed and copied.

181
00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:22.215
The Zero Trust Assessment needs PowerShell
7 and can take more than 24 hours.

182
00:12:22.215 --> 00:12:26.610
And install the program's toolkit
only from its own repository:

183
00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:31.110
a package named fmp on the public
Python index is unrelated.

184
00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:35.609
Most agencies run ScubaGear,
save the HTML report,

185
00:12:35.609 --> 00:12:38.260
and reopen it when an assessor asks.

186
00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:43.433
The program instead treats each tool as an
adapter, one input to one evidence chain,

187
00:12:43.433 --> 00:12:47.160
and leaves the tools exactly
as their publishers ship them.

188
00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.573
Each run flows through four stages.

189
00:12:49.573 --> 00:12:52.372
The tool writes its own unaltered output.

190
00:12:52.372 --> 00:12:55.822
Each result becomes a claim
in a provenance envelope:

191
00:12:55.822 --> 00:12:59.272
who issued it, when it was
extracted, and a lineage hash.

192
00:12:59.272 --> 00:13:02.922
Claims from one interval are
assembled into a sealed bundle.

193
00:13:02.922 --> 00:13:05.834
And the bundle emits
OSCAL assessment results

194
00:13:05.834 --> 00:13:08.298
and plan of action and milestones items.

195
00:13:08.298 --> 00:13:16.609
Take MS.AAD.1.1v1, legacy authentication
shall be blocked, which CISA maps to CM-7.

196
00:13:16.609 --> 00:13:17.760
Say it fails.

197
00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.995
The claim's lineage hash
ties it to the raw result,

198
00:13:20.995 --> 00:13:23.098
and the bundle opens a POA&M item.

199
00:13:23.098 --> 00:13:26.875
Months later an assessor can
rebuild the bundle, check its hash,

200
00:13:26.875 --> 00:13:30.361
and walk back to the raw output
without re-running the tool.

201
00:13:30.361 --> 00:13:31.685
The limits are stated.

202
00:13:31.685 --> 00:13:35.010
ScubaGear runs on an
interval, not continuously.

203
00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:40.010
And there's no ScubaGoggles adapter
yet, so Google Workspace is a gap.

204
00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.398
Last, turn findings into a program.

205
00:13:43.398 --> 00:13:45.735
Read each result before counting it.

206
00:13:45.735 --> 00:13:47.888
Only ScubaGear's Fail and Warning,

207
00:13:47.888 --> 00:13:52.272
and the Zero Trust Assessment's
Failed and Investigate, are findings.

208
00:13:52.272 --> 00:13:55.272
A skipped check isn't
a gap and isn't a pass.

209
00:13:55.272 --> 00:13:59.124
It's absence of evidence, recorded
with the reason, which is often a

210
00:13:59.124 --> 00:14:03.547
license or a government-cloud feature
that isn't available in the boundary.

211
00:14:03.547 --> 00:14:09.252
The program scores findings with a
documented method: severity from 0 to 100,

212
00:14:09.252 --> 00:14:12.240
priority as severity divided by effort,

213
00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.410
and buckets from P1 at
50 or above down to P4.

214
00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:18.176
Quick wins go into the next change window;

215
00:14:18.176 --> 00:14:21.348
major projects go onto a
pillar owner's roadmap.

216
00:14:21.348 --> 00:14:27.747
Both tools' findings are reconciled
against the same SP 800-53 control,

217
00:14:27.747 --> 00:14:31.123
so two pieces of evidence become
one finding for one owner.

218
00:14:31.123 --> 00:14:35.943
Anything not fixed and not excepted becomes
a plan of action and milestones item,

219
00:14:35.943 --> 00:14:38.461
which the authorizing official approves.

220
00:14:38.461 --> 00:14:42.459
Drift between intervals goes to
the authorizing official too,

221
00:14:42.459 --> 00:14:44.535
not only to security operations.

222
00:14:44.535 --> 00:14:47.365
And re-run after every remediation wave,

223
00:14:47.365 --> 00:14:51.670
so a finding is closed by
evidence, not by a ticket status.

224
00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:53.795
So, four decisions.

225
00:14:53.795 --> 00:14:56.957
Name an agency-level owner
of the Zero Trust posture,

226
00:14:56.957 --> 00:15:00.045
separate from every cloud
provider relationship.

227
00:15:00.045 --> 00:15:03.775
Reconcile the network, every
cloud and the virtualization

228
00:15:03.775 --> 00:15:07.733
estate to one authoritative record
before scoring any of them.

229
00:15:07.733 --> 00:15:14.137
Close the BOD 25-01 mandatory
findings before measuring maturity,

230
00:15:14.137 --> 00:15:17.533
with Google Workspace
due December 1, 2026.

231
00:15:17.533 --> 00:15:21.427
And move every exception into
the versioned configuration file,

232
00:15:21.427 --> 00:15:25.971
with an expiration, and route each
standing one to the authorizing official.

233
00:15:25.971 --> 00:15:28.170
The next step needs no procurement.

234
00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:32.251
Find out, pillar by pillar, which
evidence can be re-run without

235
00:15:32.251 --> 00:15:36.332
the operator's help, because
every no is the adoption boundary.

236
00:15:36.332 --> 00:15:40.782
And put the assessment hosts and
accounts in the Agency Logging Plan now.

237
00:15:40.782 --> 00:15:42.459
Unify first, assess second,

238
00:15:42.459 --> 00:15:46.421
and let named people make the
decisions the evidence supports.

239
00:15:46.421 --> 00:15:50.032
Book 11 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

240
00:15:50.032 --> 00:15:50.860
Thank you.
